rrSrSrSrSrSrSSSSSSSS.rSSSSSS .r\R)␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀5␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀V␀s/␀s␀H␀␀o␀S␀␀␀PM	␀␀␀ ␀sn␀rS!S"S#S$.rS%/␀S&QS'S(/S).S*/␀S+QSS(/S).S,.rSS-SS.SS/S0S1.rS2␀rS3␀rS4␀r␀"␀S5␀S65␀␀␀␀␀␀r␀"␀S7␀S85␀␀␀␀␀␀r␀"␀S9␀S:5␀␀␀␀␀␀r␀"␀S;␀S<5␀␀␀␀␀␀rS?S=␀jr \!S>:X␀␀a␀␀\ "␀5␀␀␀␀␀␀␀ ␀ggs ␀sn␀f␀)@a@␀␀Scripted player policy for GriddlyRTS-FoW.

The GDY only defines the rules (move / gather / attack / build_*); every decision
is made here, by the player:

    1. economy   - train workers, walk each worker to a mineral in its own corner,
                   gather ONE mineral, walk back, deposit it at the base, repeat
    2. buildings - the nearest free worker walks next to a free tile near the base
                   and builds a lab, then a barracks
    3. research  - the lab works through the player's research plan (a path through
                   the tech tree, see STRATEGIES)
    4. army      - the barracks trains the best unit the research allows (tank >
                   gunner > trooper); the army waits at a rally point
    5. offensive - once the corner is mined out and every resource has been spent,
                   the army marches on the enemy base; gunners shoot from 2 cells
                   away, everything else fights in melee

The two players follow different strategies so the research tree matters:
    player 1 (blue): Mining -> Weapons -> Gunnery  -> gunners (ranged, +1 damage)
    player 2 (red):  Mining -> Armour  -> Vehicles -> tanks   (heavy, -1 damage taken)

Both players run the same policy simultaneously: each tick, both policies read the
same game state, then all of player 1's unit actions and all of player 2's unit
actions are submitted without advancing time, and the final submission advances
the game by exactly one tick (see ``Match.step``).

The wrapper only ever issues the GDY's external actions; it never ticks internal
actions itself.
