rSrSrSrSu␀␀rrrr\S\S\S\S0r S␀r!\!"␀5␀␀␀␀␀␀␀r"S␀r#\\4S␀jr$S␀r%SS␀jr&S␀r'S␀r(\)S:X␀␀a␀␀\("␀5␀␀␀␀␀␀␀ ␀gg)a␀␀Record a demo video of GriddlyRTS-FoW and save it next to the GDY file.

Layout per frame (centered on a dark padded canvas):
    +------------------------------------------------------+
    |  (pad)                                               |
    |   P1 egocentric view        P2 egocentric view       |
    |   P1 caption (big)          P2 caption (big)         |
    |  (pad)                                               |
    |   global Block2D mini-map (centered)                 |
    |   status line (resources / carried / minerals)       |
    |  (pad)                                               |
    +------------------------------------------------------+

The egocentric view for each player is a 9x9 tile window cropped in Python
from that player's full Sprite2D render, centered on the player's camera
position (tracked via the per-player cam_x / cam_y globals that the
move_camera action updates), so the window follows the camera.

All behaviour is scripted manually from this wrapper via location-based
actions (the game has NO automatic unit behaviour):
  - move_camera [x, y, dir] pans a player's camera (arrow-key action ids)
  - build_worker [cam, 1] at the camera: -5 resources, worker spawns after 10 ticks
  - move [x, y, dir] walks a worker step by step
  - gather [x, y, dir-toward-mineral] picks up exactly ONE mineral
    (carried 0 -> 1; a second gather is blocked by the precondition)
  - gather [x, y, dir-toward-own-base] deposits it (carried 1 -> 0, +1 resource)

Output: GriddlyRTS-FoW-demo.mp4 in this directory (next to GriddlyRTS-FoW.yaml).

Run with the griddly package importable:
